-- ══ October 2026 (2026-10) ══
values ('2026-10', 'October', 2026, '2026-11-01', 57.93, 16227, 28013, 11786, 7003, 3445, 998, '61.49%', 9, 848, 335, 513, 332, 3, 10639, 10, 10, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1970, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 48.7, 53.3, 54, 6587, 4243, 2418, 4243, 356, 364, 3113, 1276, '[{"section": "Executive Summary", "label": "The Bottom Line", "type": "bottom", "text": "October closed at 57.9% of target — 16,227 of 28,013 bags, 11,786 bags short. Unposted stock (4,243 bags) explains only 36.0% of that — the rest is sales pace."}, {"section": "Executive Summary", "label": "Key point", "type": "bullet", "text": "39.2% of Kenya stock (4,243 bags) was never posted — yet posted bags sold at 48.7% of expectation. Marketing is the lever, and much of it went unused.Collaborate→ Collaborate: Post the 39.2% of Kenya stock left unmarketed — posted bags already sell at 48.7% of expectation."}, {"section": "Executive Summary", "label": "Key point", "type": "bullet", "text": "Weekly output (~3,445 bags) ran below the 7,003/week floor needed to hit target — the problem is consistency, not a bad month.Collaborate→ Collaborate: Hold a hard 7,003/wk commit reviewed every Monday; recover ~1,779/wk to close the gap."}, {"section": "Executive Summary", "label": "Key point", "type": "bullet", "text": "Regions convert about the same: Kenya posts at 48.7% sales-achieved, Sinza 53.3%, Uganda 54.0%. Posting yields within 5 points of each other — the gap is what gets posted, not where.Collaborate→ Collaborate: Lift every region''s posting yield (best Uganda 54.0%) by pointing posts at bags that convert."}, {"section": "Executive Summary", "label": "Key point", "type": "bullet", "text": "Why stock isn''t moving, and how to sell it: 4,243 units were never posted (not seen) and 497 posted units sold under half their expected (seen, not bought). See section 5 for the November plan.Collaborate→ Collaborate: Post the biggest never-posted lines first; move the non-converters onto an offer; plan the rest for November."}, {"section": "Current Performance", "label": "The Bottom Line", "type": "bottom", "text": "We finished October at 57.9% of target (16,227 bags), 11,786 bags short. Weekly output averaged about 3,445 bags — under the 7,003-bag weekly floor the target requires."}, {"section": "Current Performance", "label": "The Insight", "type": "insight", "text": "Weekly output ran below the bare minimum needed to reach target most weeks. The shortfall is one of throughput and consistency, not demand — corporate orders (998 bags) only nudged the forecast to 61.49%."}, {"section": "Current Performance", "label": "Business Impact", "type": "impact", "text": "Closing even half the weekly gap (~1,779 bags/week) across the month adds ~7,116 bags — lifting achievement from 57.9% to ~83.3%."}, {"section": "New Products", "label": "The Bottom Line", "type": "bottom", "text": "The 9 new products (LOOP BP, ZULA, LAMORA, TAJI, IMANI, NALA, AMORA, VOYAGE, LAFEMME) reached only 39.5% of their 848-bag target (335 sold) — a 513-bag deficit."}, {"section": "New Products", "label": "The Insight", "type": "insight", "text": "New products received 10,639 marketing posts but converted just 335 bags, and almost all of it (332 of 335) was Kenya — outside-Kenya is effectively untested (3 bags). Awareness is being created; conversion and distribution are the bottleneck."}, {"section": "New Products", "label": "Business Impact", "type": "impact", "text": "Recovering half the deficit (~256 bags) is realistic within one cycle; a working outside-Kenya channel would add a comparable second stream."}, {"section": "Timed Offers", "label": "On offer?", "type": "insight", "text": "All six were genuinely on offer — real list-price cuts of KES 400–1,400 (15–39% off): Mini Zuri −1,000 (-38%), Fabela −400 (-17%), Aurora −600 (-29%), Big Man Bag −600 (-29%), Zuri −1,400 (-39%), Avana HB −600 (-29%), Neo Man −400 (-25%), Gym Bag −600 (-23%), Double Press −600 (-23%), Pioneer −400 (-15%)."}, {"section": "Timed Offers", "label": "Discount vs demand", "type": "insight", "text": "Because every bag was cut, the discount and back-to-school timing can''t be fully separated — both applied at once. But discount depth didn''t decide the winners: the deepest cut, Zuri (-39%), sold only 35, while Mini Zuri led with 88 on a shallower -38% cut. The cut opened the door, but demand — which bags people wanted for school — chose what actually moved."}, {"section": "Timed Offers", "label": "Stock", "type": "insight", "text": "Real inventory behind them — 2,092 still in stock (led by Fabela at 411). The offer draws down a genuine stock position, not a token one."}, {"section": "Timed Offers", "label": "Season", "type": "insight", "text": "All six sit in Backpack, Handbag, Man Bag, Sling, Sport, Travel — school categories — and the step-up landed in the offer week as schools opened. Aurora and Zuri even sold on zero marketing posts (pure demand). The timing fits the sales pattern as much as the price cut does."}, {"section": "Timed Offers", "label": "Corporate order", "type": "insight", "text": "Fabela shows -61% because of a bulk / corporate order: 440 × Fabela Grey (HILTON/99665, HILTON, 2026-10-05, KES 1,012,001) before the offer — it inflated the before-offer pace, so the offer looks worse than it was. Without it, Fabela did +71% (walk-in pace only)."}, {"section": "Timed Offers", "label": "On offer?", "type": "insight", "text": "All six were genuinely on offer — real list-price cuts of KES 400–1,000 (15–38% off): Fabela −400 (-17%), Mini Zuri −1,000 (-38%), Aurora −600 (-29%), Belt Bag −400 (-29%), Neo Man −400 (-25%), Big Man Bag −400 (-19%), Double Press −600 (-23%), Trecento −400 (-19%), Pioneer −400 (-15%), Gym Bag −400 (-15%)."}, {"section": "Timed Offers", "label": "Discount vs demand", "type": "insight", "text": "Because every bag was cut, the discount and back-to-school timing can''t be fully separated — both applied at once. But discount depth didn''t decide the winners: the deepest cut, Mini Zuri (-38%), sold only 46, while Fabela led with 91 on a shallower -17% cut. The cut opened the door, but demand — which bags people wanted for school — chose what actually moved."}, {"section": "Timed Offers", "label": "Stock", "type": "insight", "text": "Real inventory behind them — 2,084 still in stock (led by Fabela at 411). The offer draws down a genuine stock position, not a token one."}, {"section": "Timed Offers", "label": "Season", "type": "insight", "text": "All six sit in Backpack, Handbag, Man Bag, Sling, Sport, Travel, Waist Bag — school categories — and the step-up landed in the offer week as schools opened. Aurora and Belt Bag even sold on zero marketing posts (pure demand). The timing fits the sales pattern as much as the price cut does."}, {"section": "Timed Offers", "label": "Corporate order", "type": "insight", "text": "Fabela shows -3% because of a bulk / corporate order: 440 × Fabela Grey (HILTON/99665, HILTON, 2026-10-05, KES 1,012,001) before the offer; 10 × Fabela Black (KISUMU/16363, KISUMU, 2026-10-16, KES 25,000) during the offer; 10 × Fabela Green (KISUMU/16363, KISUMU, 2026-10-16, KES 25,000) during the offer; 10 × Fabela Black (KISUMU/16364, KISUMU, 2026-10-16, KES 25,000) during the offer; 10 × Fabela Green (KISUMU/16364, KISUMU, 2026-10-16, KES 25,000) during the offer — it sits in both windows and distorts the comparison. Without it, Fabela did +63% (walk-in pace only)."}, {"section": "Timed Offers", "label": "On offer?", "type": "insight", "text": "All six were genuinely on offer — real list-price cuts of KES 400–600 (15–29% off): Fabela −400 (-17%), Big Man Bag −400 (-19%), Neo Man −400 (-25%), Belt Bag −400 (-29%), Gym Bag −400 (-15%), Fayola −600 (-29%), Liam −400 (-19%), Double Press −600 (-23%), Pioneer −400 (-15%), Avana HB −600 (-29%)."}, {"section": "Timed Offers", "label": "Discount vs demand", "type": "insight", "text": "Because every bag was cut, the discount and back-to-school timing can''t be fully separated — both applied at once. But discount depth didn''t decide the winners: the deepest cut, Belt Bag (-29%), sold only 47, while Fabela led with 99 on a shallower -17% cut. The cut opened the door, but demand — which bags people wanted for school — chose what actually moved."}, {"section": "Timed Offers", "label": "Stock", "type": "insight", "text": "Real inventory behind them — 2,152 still in stock (led by Fabela at 411). The offer draws down a genuine stock position, not a token one."}, {"section": "Timed Offers", "label": "Season", "type": "insight", "text": "All six sit in Backpack, Man Bag, Messenger, Sling, Sport, Travel, Waist Bag — school categories — and the step-up landed in the offer week as schools opened. Belt Bag and Gym Bag even sold on zero marketing posts (pure demand). The timing fits the sales pattern as much as the price cut does."}, {"section": "Timed Offers", "label": "Corporate order", "type": "insight", "text": "Fabela shows -61% because of a bulk / corporate order: 440 × Fabela Grey (HILTON/99665, HILTON, 2026-10-05, KES 1,012,001) before the offer; 10 × Fabela Black (KISUMU/16363, KISUMU, 2026-10-16, KES 25,000) before the offer; 10 × Fabela Green (KISUMU/16363, KISUMU, 2026-10-16, KES 25,000) before the offer; 10 × Fabela Black (KISUMU/16364, KISUMU, 2026-10-16, KES 25,000) before the offer; 10 × Fabela Green (KISUMU/16364, KISUMU, 2026-10-16, KES 25,000) before the offer — it inflated the before-offer pace, so the offer looks worse than it was. Without it, Fabela did +4% (walk-in pace only)."}, {"section": "Offer Type Analysis", "label": "The Bottom Line", "type": "bottom", "text": "Across 10 combos and 10 power deals (Kenya), the two offer types cleared stock very differently: combos moved 0 bags (KES 0) while power deals moved 0 bags (KES 0). Combos shifted the most stock; combos made the most money."}, {"section": "Offer Type Analysis", "label": "The Insight", "type": "insight", "text": "It''s a price-point story: combos averaged KES 0 per offer vs power deals KES 1,970. The cheaper combos clear more volume, so dead stock moves fastest through combos — yet 3,883 bags remain in Kenya offer stock (plus 1,706 Sinza, 1,304 Uganda), so the mix still isn''t drawing inventory down fast enough."}, {"section": "Offer Type Analysis", "label": "Sinza & Uganda offers", "type": "insight", "text": "Sinza ran 10 combos, 10 singles and 0 specials, moving 0 bags (value 0) against 1,706 in stock. Uganda ran 5 combos and 5 singles, moving 0 bags (value 0) against 1,304 in stock. Both are clearing only a thin slice of their offer stock — the same dead-stock problem as Kenya, at smaller scale."}, {"section": "Offer Type Analysis", "label": "Business Impact", "type": "impact", "text": "Clearing just 20% of the 3,883-bag Kenya position (~777 bags) in the month is ~2.8% of target — and frees working capital tied up in slow stock."}, {"section": "Self-Made Combos", "label": "The Bottom Line", "type": "bottom", "text": "Staff-made combos were 148 combos moving 151 units (KES 750,895) — 16% of all combo volume — vs 10 official running combos moving 822 units (KES 3,602,496). 177 combo requests were logged this month. 2 request(s) were rejected (e.g. CBR/2026/0899 — Not matching just correct)."}, {"section": "Self-Made Combos", "label": "The Insight", "type": "insight", "text": "Self-made combos average KES 4,973 vs running combos KES 4,383, so the running combos are the cheaper ticket. Every self-made combo needs an approved CBR, so the request table behind them is the audit trail — who requested what, from which shop, and whether it actually sold."}, {"section": "Posting Yields (Sales from Accurate Posting)", "label": "The Bottom Line", "type": "bottom", "text": "Marketing posting is our most effective lever — Kenya posted bags hit 48.7% of expected sales monthly — but 39.2% of in-stock bags (4,243) were never posted. The target gap is sitting in unmarketed stock."}, {"section": "Posting Yields (Sales from Accurate Posting)", "label": "Which bags were never postedAgainst the month''s posting, these in-stock bags carry zero marketing posts — the dead stock marketing moves first so the sales team can reach target:Brown ANTITHEFT BROWN177 in stock · 0 postsBlack ANTITHEFT BLACK143 in stock · 0 postsBlack TRAVEL BLACK126 in stock · 0 postsBrown TRAVEL BROWN108 in stock · 0 postsGreen MAN BAG GREEN105 in stock · 0 postsGrey ANTITHEFT GREY103 in stock · 0 postsBrown PRIME BROWN103 in stock · 0 postsGreen BIG MAN BAG GREEN98 in stock · 0 postsBrown PIONEER BROWN94 in stock · 0 postsGreen FABELA GREEN93 in stock · 0 posts The Insight", "type": "insight", "text": "Where we post, we sell: monthly sales on posted bags (3,113) ran ~2.4× the 1,276 expected. Yet of 10,830 bags in Kenya stock, 4,243 got no post all month. Regionally the discipline collapses — Kenya 48.7%, Sinza 53.3%, Uganda 54.0% — so the biggest untapped demand is in Kenya and in unmarketed stock."}, {"section": "Posting Yields (Sales from Accurate Posting)", "label": "Business Impact", "type": "impact", "text": "If the 4,243 unposted bags converted at even a conservative 20% once marketed, that is ~849 bags — enough to close ~7.2% of the entire target gap. Bringing Kenya toward Kenya''s rate compounds it further."}, {"section": "Remaining Stock — Why It Isn''t Moving & How to Sell It", "label": "The Bottom Line", "type": "bottom", "text": "October has closed, so this is the opening plan for November. Kenya is holding 4,243 units that were never posted (92 bag lines) and 497 units that were posted but sold under half of what their posts should bring (39 lines). Posting alone can move ~849 of these early next month — so the question is which bags get the posts."}, {"section": "Remaining Stock — Why It Isn''t Moving & How to Sell It", "label": "Why — two different problems Not being seen: in stock, zero posts this month. These have never had a chance — posting is the fix. ANTITHEFT BROWN177 in stock · 0 postsANTITHEFT BLACK143 in stock · 0 postsTRAVEL BLACK126 in stock · 0 postsTRAVEL BROWN108 in stock · 0 postsMAN BAG GREEN105 in stock · 0 postsANTITHEFT GREY103 in stock · 0 postsPRIME BROWN103 in stock · 0 postsBIG MAN BAG GREEN98 in stock · 0 posts Seen but not bought: posted, yet sold under 50% of expected. More posts won''t fix these — price, colour or offer will. MAN BAG GREY38 in stock · 34 posts · 32 soldLAFEMME BLACK (OBSIDIAN)36 in stock · 46 posts · 18 soldLAMORA RED33 in stock · 59 posts · 7 soldZULA BLACK33 in stock · 39 posts · 43 soldZURI GREY32 in stock · 18 posts · 4 soldLAFEMME RED (GARNET)22 in stock · 75 posts · 3 soldJADE GREY22 in stock · 18 posts · 10 soldNALA BLUE21 in stock · 53 posts · 7 sold How — within the month At October''s pace of ~78 posts/day, each Kenya post brought in ~1.1 bags. Start November with the posts where they move the most stock: StartPost the biggest never-posted lines first (top of the red list) — they hold the most stock and have had no marketing at all. Start testingMove the \"seen but not bought\" lines onto an offer — a power deal, a combo slot (Offer Picking) or a price step — instead of re-posting them. StopSpending posts on bags that already sell without them, while these lines sit. Business Impact", "type": "insight", "text": "~4,740 units carry into November — schedule them week by week so they are posted or on an offer by mid-month."}, {"section": "Current Performance", "label": "Recommendation", "type": "rec", "text": "Managing to a hard 7,003-bag weekly commit, reviewed every Monday — not a single monthly number reconciled at month-end."}, {"section": "Current Performance", "label": "Recommendation", "type": "rec", "text": "Treating the ~3,558-bag weekly gap as normal. Each missed week is unrecoverable against a fixed monthly target."}, {"section": "New Products", "label": "Recommendation", "type": "rec", "text": "A concentrated push on the weakest launches with price/offer support to clear the 513-bag deficit."}, {"section": "New Products", "label": "Recommendation", "type": "rec", "text": "Outside-Kenya distribution for the best-performing new lines — 3 bags sold there all month is an untested market, not a dead one."}, {"section": "Offer Type Analysis", "label": "Recommendation", "type": "rec", "text": "Routing the slowest dead stock through combos (the higher-volume clearer) and keeping premium movers in power deals for margin."}, {"section": "Offer Type Analysis", "label": "Recommendation", "type": "rec", "text": "Restocking the slowest-moving colours until the 3,883-bag Kenya position draws down."}, {"section": "Posting Yields (Sales from Accurate Posting)", "label": "Focus — move the bags NOT on offerMarketing''s real target is the stock sitting not on offer. Read across the three posting lenses:CombinedSales vs Expected (Posted & Unposted) — of Kenya''s in-stock bags, 6,587 are on offer but 4,243 sit not on offer: dead stock we aren''t even putting in front of buyers.With postingSales with Posting vs Expected — where marketing posted, sales ran ~2.4× expected. Posting demonstrably works, so aiming it at the not-on-offer pile is the lever.No postingSales with no Posting vs Opportunity if Marketed — the sales team already moved 4,755 bags with zero marketing; the gap up to the opportunity is what marketing leaves on the table by not posting the not-on-offer stock.Not-on-offer stock by region: Kenya 4,243, Sinza 356, Uganda 364 — and Sinza (53.3%) and Uganda (54.0%) move it far slower than Kenya (48.7%). Same playbook, all three regions. Bags NOT on offer, by region — the dead stock marketing must move Recommendation", "type": "rec", "text": "Systematically posting the 4,243 unmarketed in-stock bags — the single largest, cheapest lever against the 11,786-bag target gap."}, {"section": "Posting Yields (Sales from Accurate Posting)", "label": "Focus — move the bags NOT on offerMarketing''s real target is the stock sitting not on offer. Read across the three posting lenses:CombinedSales vs Expected (Posted & Unposted) — of Kenya''s in-stock bags, 6,587 are on offer but 4,243 sit not on offer: dead stock we aren''t even putting in front of buyers.With postingSales with Posting vs Expected — where marketing posted, sales ran ~2.4× expected. Posting demonstrably works, so aiming it at the not-on-offer pile is the lever.No postingSales with no Posting vs Opportunity if Marketed — the sales team already moved 4,755 bags with zero marketing; the gap up to the opportunity is what marketing leaves on the table by not posting the not-on-offer stock.Not-on-offer stock by region: Kenya 4,243, Sinza 356, Uganda 364 — and Sinza (53.3%) and Uganda (54.0%) move it far slower than Kenya (48.7%). Same playbook, all three regions. Bags NOT on offer, by region — the dead stock marketing must move Recommendation", "type": "rec", "text": "The Uganda posting playbook in Kenya, where sales-achieved is 48.7% vs Uganda''s 54.0%."}, {"section": "Posting Yields (Sales from Accurate Posting)", "label": "Focus — move the bags NOT on offerMarketing''s real target is the stock sitting not on offer. Read across the three posting lenses:CombinedSales vs Expected (Posted & Unposted) — of Kenya''s in-stock bags, 6,587 are on offer but 4,243 sit not on offer: dead stock we aren''t even putting in front of buyers.With postingSales with Posting vs Expected — where marketing posted, sales ran ~2.4× expected. Posting demonstrably works, so aiming it at the not-on-offer pile is the lever.No postingSales with no Posting vs Opportunity if Marketed — the sales team already moved 4,755 bags with zero marketing; the gap up to the opportunity is what marketing leaves on the table by not posting the not-on-offer stock.Not-on-offer stock by region: Kenya 4,243, Sinza 356, Uganda 364 — and Sinza (53.3%) and Uganda (54.0%) move it far slower than Kenya (48.7%). Same playbook, all three regions. Bags NOT on offer, by region — the dead stock marketing must move Recommendation", "type": "rec", "text": "Letting in-stock bags sit unposted — every unmarketed bag is a bag that reliably does not sell."}]'::jsonb, '{"deals": {"powerCount": 10, "powerSold": 4453, "powerRevenue": 9031689, "powerDisc": 4800, "powerSoldExCombo": 1214, "dowProducts": 75, "dowRows": 300, "dowLocations": 17, "dowSold": 1951, "dowRevenue": 3582300, "dowDisc": 29700, "dowSoldExCombo": 1575, "dedupSold": 6381, "dedupRevenue": 12579589, "dedupProducts": 48, "overlapProducts": 37, "comboBagDeals": 12, "tiers": [{"tier": "1", "products": 31, "units": 708, "revenue": 1279800, "discount": 282200, "weeksElapsed": 2, "inProgress": false, "runRate": 354.0, "label": "Tier 1", "window": "Wk 1–2"}, {"tier": "2", "products": 27, "units": 789, "revenue": 1473300, "discount": 310000, "weeksElapsed": 2, "inProgress": false, "runRate": 394.5, "label": "Tier 2", "window": "Wk 3–4"}, {"tier": "3", "products": 17, "units": 454, "revenue": 813000, "discount": 179500, "weeksElapsed": 1, "inProgress": true, "runRate": 454.0, "label": "Regional", "window": "From 25 Oct"}], "power": [{"product": "Code 3", "tier": "All", "orig": 2100, "now": 1800, "disc": 300, "sold": 899, "revenue": 1620336, "stock": 562}, {"product": "Antitheft", "tier": "All", "orig": 2600, "now": 2200, "disc": 400, "sold": 823, "revenue": 1808800, "stock": 581}, {"product": "Jumbo", "tier": "All", "orig": 3600, "now": 2800, "disc": 800, "sold": 626, "revenue": 1753290, "stock": 549}, {"product": "Standard Travel", "tier": "All", "orig": 2600, "now": 2200, "disc": 400, "sold": 423, "revenue": 933060, "stock": 415}, {"product": "Prime", "tier": "All", "orig": 2800, "now": 2400, "disc": 400, "sold": 381, "revenue": 919203, "stock": 164}, {"product": "Aurora", "tier": "All", "orig": 2100, "now": 1700, "disc": 400, "sold": 328, "revenue": 539300, "stock": 158}, {"product": "Aria Sling", "tier": "All", "orig": 1600, "now": 1200, "disc": 400, "sold": 304, "revenue": 365600, "stock": 116}, {"product": "Moon Bag", "tier": "All", "orig": 1600, "now": 1200, "disc": 400, "sold": 260, "rev